The proposed Web-based Centralized Faculty Evaluation System for Benedicto College was checked to see if it can be developed and used with the resources available to the researchers. This feasibility study focuses on three areas: technical feasibility, financial feasibility, and operational feasibility. These areas were considered to determine if the system can be developed properly, if the expenses will be manageable, and if the system can be used to improve the current faculty evaluation process. The proposed system is technically feasible because the researchers have the needed tools and resources for its development. The system will be developed as a web-based application using Next.js, React, TypeScript, Vite, and Tailwind CSS for the frontend. For the backend, Node.js and Express.js will be used to handle requests and connect the different parts of the system. MySQL will be used to store the evaluation responses, faculty information, user accounts, and other records needed by the system.
The researchers will also use Visual Studio Code for writing and managing the system code. EchoAPI will be used to test the APIs and check if the frontend and backend are communicating properly. GitHub will be used to save the source code and help the researchers work together on the project. The hardware available to the researchers is also enough for developing and testing the system. The computer has an AMD Ryzen 5 processor, 16GB RAM, 512GB SSD, and Windows 11 Pro. These specifications can handle the development tools and technologies needed for the project. An internet connection will also be needed when developing, testing, and accessing the web-based system. Since the required technologies, development tools, and hardware are already available, the proposed system is considered technically feasible.
Financially feasible because the project does not require a large amount of money for       software and hardware. Most of the technologies that will be used are free or open-source. These include Next.js, React, TypeScript, Node.js, Express.js, MySQL, and Tailwind CSS. The researchers can also use Visual Studio Code and GitHub without needing to purchase expensive software licenses. The researchers already have computers that can be used for the development and testing of the system. Because of this, there is no need to purchase additional specialized hardware. The main expenses related to the project are the internet connection and electricity used during development and testing. Overall, the project can be developed using the resources that are already available to the researchers. Since there are only minimal additional expenses, the proposed system is considered financially feasible.
Operationally feasible because it is designed to improve the current faculty evaluation process used by the institution. The system will mainly be used by students and authorized administrators. Students will be able to access the evaluation form through the web-based system and submit their evaluations of faculty members. The evaluation will follow the criteria approved by the institution. This will provide students with one place where they can complete and submit their evaluations. For administrators, the system will provide a centralized place for managing evaluation records. They will be able to access evaluation responses, manage records, monitor evaluations, and generate reports. The Principal or Dean may also use the system to evaluate the faculty members under their respective department, this will allow them to provide evaluations based on the approved criteria and help monitor the performance of faculty members within their department. This can reduce the need to manually evaluate faculty members using paper forms and manually calculate their average evaluation scores, since the Principal or Dean can conduct their evaluations directly through the system instead of using paper-based evaluations. The system can also make evaluation records easier to access and manage because the information will be stored in one database. This addresses some of the problems identified in the current process, particularly the organization of responses, centralized data management, accessibility of results, and time required to process evaluation records.
Faculty members may also benefit from the system because the organized evaluation results can help them see their strengths and the areas where they may improve their teaching. However, the system will not be used for faculty promotion, payroll, attendance, or other human resource activities, as stated in the scope and limitations of the study. Based on these considerations, the proposed system is considered operationally feasible because it can support the existing faculty evaluation process while making the management of evaluation records more organized and convenient. Based on the three areas examined, the Web-based Centralized Faculty Evaluation System for Benedicto College is considered feasible. The researchers have the necessary hardware, software, and technologies to develop the system. The project also requires only minimal additional expenses since most of the development tools are free and the researchers already have the needed computers. The system is also suitable for the current faculty evaluation process. It can give students a convenient way to submit their evaluations and provide administrators with a centralized place to manage evaluation records and generate reports. By addressing the problems found in the current Google Forms-based process, the proposed system can help make faculty evaluation more organized and efficient. Therefore, based on its technical, financial, and operational aspects, the proposed Web-based Centralized Faculty Evaluation System is considered feasible for development and implementation at Benedicto College.
